From 78c404033aab80085a8b664b5278436dde084670 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Neil Johnson <njohnson@roku.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 13:41:12 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 04/26] aslink: fix DefineSDCDB() hanging on any symbol
 containing a '$'

DefineSDCDB() in lksdcdb.c decides whether a symbol belongs in the
SDCDB .cdb output by counting the '$' characters in its name - the
comment directly above the loop reads "SDC symbols have 3 or more $
characters". The scan never advances past a match:

	j = 0;
	p1 = name;
	while ((p1 = strchr(p1, '$')) != NULL) {
		j += 1;
	}

strchr() returns a pointer to the first '$' at or after p1, and that
pointer is assigned straight back to p1. On the next iteration
strchr() is handed a pointer that already points at a '$', so it
returns the same address again, and again, incrementing j forever.
The loop terminates only for a name containing no '$' at all - that
is, only for the names the function exists to filter out. Every name
it was written to match hangs the linker.

The effect is that 'aslink -y' never terminates on any real SDCC
input: SDCC's mangled symbol names ("F$main$0$0", "G$counter$0$0$1")
carry a '$' between every field, so the very first such symbol
reaching DefineSDCDB() spins indefinitely with no output and no
diagnostic. Nothing else is affected - DefineSDCDB() is reached only
from lstarea() in lklist.c, and only when yflag is set - so a link
without -y is entirely unaffected, which is why the fault has stayed
latent.

Advance p1 past each match, so the scan progresses and terminates:

	while ((p1 = strchr(p1, '$')) != NULL) {
		p1 += 1;
		j += 1;
	}

The counting semantics are otherwise unchanged: j is the number of
'$' characters in the name, and the existing "j > 2" test continues
to select names with three or more of them.

Verified with a standalone aslink built from linksrc/*.c:
- Reproduced with a module exporting five symbols spanning the
  decision boundary - "plain_sym" (no '$'), "one$dollar" (one),
  "two$dol$lars" (two), "F$main$0$0" (three) and "G$counter$0$0$1"
  (four). The unpatched linker run as "aslink -n -y -i" produces no
  output and does not terminate (killed at a 10 second timeout); the
  patched linker completes immediately.
- Confirmed the boundary is still honoured after the fix: the
  resulting .cdb contains exactly the two names with three or more
  '$' characters, "L:F$main$0$0:0" and "L:G$counter$0$0$1:1", and
  none of the three below the threshold.
- Confirmed no change when -y is not in use: linked a two-module
  test with and without the patch, producing byte-identical Intel
  Hex output and byte-identical .map files (timestamps aside).
- Ran the existing asz80 self-tests, tz80.bat and t80.bat
  (asz80 -gloaxf, aslink -xu), against the unpatched and patched
  linker: byte-for-byte identical .lst and .rst output in both
  cases. The assembler-side "Not an 8080 instruction" reports in
  t80 are pre-existing and unrelated, and appear unchanged in both
  runs. No regressions.
- Rebuilt with -fsanitize=address,undefined and reran both the -y
  and the no--y links: no sanitizer reports.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 linksrc/lksdcdb.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/linksrc/lksdcdb.c b/linksrc/lksdcdb.c
index 6b30099..7b1fbe3 100644
--- a/linksrc/lksdcdb.c
+++ b/linksrc/lksdcdb.c
@@ -163,6 +163,7 @@ void DefineSDCDB(char *name, a_uint value)
 	j = 0;
 	p1 = name;
 	while ((p1 = strchr(p1, '$')) != NULL) {
+		p1 += 1;
 		j += 1;
 	}
 
